        {"t": "note", "title": "Isolated worker", "text": "New functions target the isolated worker model. Your function code runs in a separate process from the host. That is the model that moves forward with .NET 10. The older in-process model, where the function shared the host's CLR, is the legacy path. In an interview, say isolated worker, say why cold start exists, and say which plan you would buy for the latency you need."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "App Service or Functions for an order API that also sends email?",
         "a": "The HTTP API goes on App Service: it has steady traffic, it needs a stable latency, and it owns the request. The email goes on a queue. A function triggered by Service Bus sends the email and completes the message. If email is slow or the provider is down, the API has already committed the order and returned 201. The function retries with the Service Bus delivery count. I do not send email inside the POST, and I do not put the whole CRUD API on a Consumption function if the product expects hot latency all day. A function is a fine host for a small HTTP API with spiky traffic if cold start is acceptable or the plan keeps instances warm.",
         "lead": "I split by scaling and failure, not by technology fashion. The API and the email worker scale for different reasons. If they share a process, a mail-provider outage and a retry loop can starve the API threads."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "How do deployment slots give you a safer release?",
         "a": "A staging slot runs the new bits with production-like settings. I warm it up, I hit the health endpoint, and I run a smoke check. Swap exchanges the slots. The old bits remain on the staging slot for a swap-back. Connection strings and app settings can be sticky to a slot, and I am deliberate about that: a staging slot must not point at a destructive migration by accident, and it must not keep a staging secret after swap unless I marked it sticky on purpose. Always On stays on for the API so the process is there. Health checks on the App Service should hit readiness, including a lightweight dependency check, so traffic does not land on a bad instance. Migrations run as a pipeline step before traffic moves, using the expand/contract rule from Day 4.",
         "lead": "A swap is not a migration strategy by itself. If the new code requires a column the old code cannot read, swap-back is a lie. I only call a release reversible when the previous bits still work against the current schema."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "How does the API get a secret and reach Azure SQL without a password?",
         "a": "The App Service has a system-assigned or user-assigned managed identity. I grant that identity access to Key Vault secrets it needs, and I reference those secrets from app settings so they appear as configuration. For Azure SQL I create a user for that identity and add it to the runtime database role. The app uses Azure identity credentials in the SQL client. There is no SQL password and no secret in source control. Locally, developers use their own Entra login or a dev database with a user secret stored outside git. Key rotation becomes an identity story, not a password-mailing story. I still validate options on startup so a missing vault reference fails the slot before swap.",
         "lead": "I can draw three identities: the user's token, the API's managed identity, and the pipeline's migration identity. If I cannot name all three, the design is not finished."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "What is a cold start, and which plan do you choose?",
         "a": "On the Consumption plan, Azure can scale to zero. The next invocation loads the host and your isolated worker, and that delay is the cold start. Premium and dedicated plans keep instances warm, with a higher floor cost. I choose Consumption for low-volume jobs, scheduled cleanup, and work that can wait a second. I choose Premium or an App Service plan when a user is waiting, or when the function must hold a connection and run longer with predictable throughput. I also watch the timeout. A function that processes a large file in one invocation will hit the plan limit. I split the file into messages or I move that workload to a host with a duration that matches the job.",
         "lead": "I put the plan in the design doc with the latency target. Choosing Consumption because it is cheap, then discovering checkout waits on a cold start, is a lead miss. Cost is part of the decision, and so is the user waiting."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "Walk a Service Bus trigger from delivery to completion.",
         "a": "Service Bus delivers a message under a peek lock. The lock is a lease, not a deletion. The function runs. If it finishes, it completes the message and Service Bus removes it. If it throws, the message is abandoned and becomes visible again after the lock, up to MaxDeliveryCount. After that it is dead-lettered. I inspect the dead-letter reason, fix the poison or the bug, and have a replay path. I renew the lock, or I keep the work shorter than the lock, so a long job is not processed twice by another worker who received it after the lock expired. The handler is idempotent: if the same message arrives twice, the database unique key or a processed-message table stops a double charge. I set prefetch carefully. A huge prefetch on a slow handler holds messages that another instance could have finished.",
         "lead": "I can describe the poison message from my own system, or a realistic one: a payload that always throws, burns the delivery count, and sits on the dead-letter queue while the rest of the queue stays healthy. The lead's job is the alert on dead-letter depth and the replay runbook, not only the happy complete."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "What belongs in a Durable Functions orchestration?",
         "a": "An orchestration coordinates. It can wait for events, fan out activities, and run compensations. It must be deterministic: no direct IO, no DateTime.Now, no random GUID inside the orchestrator. Those go in activities, and the orchestrator calls them through the context. State is checkpointed, so a replay must make the same decisions. I use Durable Functions when the workflow is long, has human waits, or needs a clear compensation owner, such as reserve inventory, take payment, and release inventory if payment fails. I use a single message and a database row when the workflow is one step. I do not hide a simple retry policy inside an orchestration.",
         "lead": "Determinism is the question that separates someone who read the feature from someone who has operated it. I mention replay explicitly. If the interviewer has not used Durable Functions, I still show I know when a row and a queue are enough."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "What breaks when you scale from one instance to ten?",
         "a": "Anything in static fields or in-memory cache becomes ten copies, possibly inconsistent. A background timer inside the API runs ten times unless I use a distributed lock or move it to a single scheduled function. A file on local disk is not shared. Session affinity on App Service hides the mistake and then sticks users to dead instances, so a stateless API turns affinity off. SQL connections scale with instances, so ten instances with a fat pool can exhaust Azure SQL. I set the pool size with the max instances in mind. A singleton lock still only locks one process. Distributed coordination uses SQL, Service Bus sessions, or a purpose-built store.",
         "lead": "I ask every design: what happens on the second instance? If the answer is we only run one, I ask what happens when the platform restarts that one. Single instance is a availability decision, and I want it on paper."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "Production is slow right now. What do you open first?",
         "a": "I start from the user symptom and the correlation id, then the request chart: rate, latency, errors, and dependency time. If SQL time dominates, I go to Query Store and blocking, not to a code deploy. If an outbound HTTP dependency dominates, I check timeout, retry storms, and the circuit breaker. If CPU is high on the App Service and SQL is fine, I look for a hot loop, bad cache, or thread-pool starvation from blocking calls. I check recent deploys and a slot swap time. I decide rollback versus fix-forward with a clock: if the last deploy lines up and rollback is safe, I swap back first and diagnose second. I communicate a one-line status while I do it. Afterward I write the cause, the customer impact, and the guard that would have caught it.",
         "lead": "The panel is listening for sequence and calm. Metrics, a reversible action, then root cause. A lead who opens Visual Studio and reads code for twenty minutes while the slot can be swapped back has the wrong order."},
